<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sao Hkun Hseng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sao Hkun Hseng was saopha, or prince, of Hsipaw, a northern Shan state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, from 1886. Coming to power as the British annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, he cooperated with the new rulers and became one of the most important Shan chiefs under British protection. He worked to modernise his state, which lay on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div>
